    body: `<div class="wk">${sec(0, 'Today’s')}${sec(1, 'Tomorrow’s')}</div>
<div class="prose"><h2>What “single shot” means on ZurePicks</h2><p>It is one game and one outcome, bet on its own. No stacking, no “just add one more leg”. Every day we name a shot of the day and a backup, and that is the whole list. We hold the line at two games because a longer list stops being a pick and turns into a menu.</p><p>The price is the other fixed point. We aim at 2.00, so a winning 100 stake comes back as 200. If you want more return than that, the honest route is the <a href="3-odds-predictions.html">3 odds slip</a>, not a longer single price. Higher single prices mean a lower chance, and the chance is the part we care about.</p>
<h2>How the pick is chosen</h2><p>${nGames(0) ? `For today we have ${nGames(0)} games with prices. ` : ''}${nGames(1) ? `Tomorrow has ${nGames(1)} so far, and that number grows as bookmakers publish more. ` : ''}For each game that has not kicked off we read every market we hold: match result, double chance, over and under on 1.5 and 2.5 goals, both teams to score, and a handful of extras such as team goals, first-half goals and draw no bet.</p><p>Bookmaker prices carry a margin, so the raw odds overstate every outcome a little. We strip that margin out, which leaves the chance the market really believes in. Then we keep only outcomes priced between 1.90 and 2.20. Only if fewer than two games qualify do we widen that to 1.85 to 2.30. Of what is left, the highest chance wins, one outcome per game. ${now0}</p>
<h2>Why the chance is only around 50%</h2><p>This surprises people, so it is worth saying plainly. Odds of 2.00 mean the market thinks the outcome happens about half the time. After the bookmaker’s margin, the best you can find near that price is usually 48 to 52%. A single at 2.00 is a close call by construction. What we do is find the best-priced close call of the day, not a safe one. If you want safer, use <a href="2-odds-predictions.html">2 odds</a> built from shorter-priced games, and accept that a 70% leg still loses three times in ten.</p>
<h2>How the shot has done so far</h2>${REC.n ? `<p>We replayed every day we hold results for. That is ${REC.days} days, using the pre-match prices saved for each one. The shot of the day won ${REC.w} of ${REC.n} (${pct}%) and the backup won ${REC.b.w} of ${REC.b.n} (${bpct}%). At a flat stake on the shot of the day that comes to ${REC.profit >= 0 ? '+' : '−'}${Math.abs(REC.profit).toFixed(1)} units. It is a small sample and we are not hiding behind that, because a few more weeks could move it either way. Prices near 2.00 are a coin flip with a margin attached, and any honest record will look like this for a while.</p><div class="sc"><table><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Game</th><th>Pick</th><th>Odds</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>${rows}</tbody></table></div>` : `<p>Results build up as days finish. Once there are a few weeks of shots, the record will sit here, wins and losses together.</p>`}
<h2>Single or accumulator?</h2><p>Take two games with a 71% chance each. Put them in one slip and the price comes to about 2.0, but you need both to land, and the combined chance is 50%. A single at 2.00 also sits near 50%. So the chance is about the same, and what changes is the way you can lose. In the slip, either game can sink you. In the single, there is only one game to watch. If your problem is a ticket that dies at 89 minutes in the fourth leg, the single removes it. The <a href="how-accumulator-bets-work.html">accumulators guide</a> goes through the arithmetic.</p>
<h2>Using it without getting hurt</h2><p>Decide the stake before you open the page, and make it an amount you could lose without it mattering. Do not chase a loss with a bigger single the next day. The shot of the day is not a signal to bet more. 18+ only. If gambling is starting to feel like a problem, <a href="responsible-gambling.html">get help here</a>.</p></div>`,
